# A "plain string literal" guard — reject anything that is not a bare single-quoted
# literal before eval/exec — means only a quoted string literal is ever evaluated, which
# returns a string and executes no code. The guard's startswith() call dominates the sink
# (early return), so CodeExecutionValidation covers the endpoint. Without an unguarded
# reassignment the code-injection finding must not fire; the unguarded variant still must.

test "python codeinj: quoted-string-literal guard before eval neutralizes the sink"
  lang python
  reject VYQL-INJ-003
  code
  ```
  def handler(request):
      param = request.form.get("q")
      bar = param
      if not bar.startswith("'") or not bar.endswith("'") or "'" in bar[1:-1]:
          return "Eval argument must be a plain string literal."
      return eval(bar)
  ```

test "python codeinj: tainted value reaching eval with no guard is vulnerable"
  lang python
  expect VYQL-INJ-003
  code
  ```
  def handler(request):
      param = request.form.get("q")
      bar = param
      return eval(bar)
  ```
